00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.470
- That's clearly on, hello.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.958
I'm Tiffany Li and I'm a resident fellow

00:01:57.958 --> 00:02:01.640
at the law school's
Information Society Project,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.840
and there I lead an initiative
called the Wikimedia and

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.730
Yale Law School Initiative

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:09.020
on Intermediaries and Information.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.870
It's a very long name for
a very simple concept,

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:15.030
which is that I conduct
research and have events

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.413
and programming along the
topics of open internet,

00:02:18.413 --> 00:02:21.070
open knowledge, open access.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.560
Specifically a lot of my
legal research focuses

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.830
on intermediary liability, or
the rules for liability for

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:30.326
platforms, tech platforms, like Facebook,

00:02:30.326 --> 00:02:32.983
or Google, or Twitter.

00:02:32.983 --> 00:02:35.490
So when I think about
interdisciplinary research

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.911
or open knowledge, or new
ways of thinking about

00:02:37.911 --> 00:02:41.719
humanities or the social
sciences, a lot of my knowledge

00:02:41.719 --> 00:02:43.905
is filtered through my experiences.

00:02:43.905 --> 00:02:47.400
And law is not really
what you may think of as a

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.740
discipline that is very open
to interdisciplinary research.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.800
We don't have any fun projects
with succulents for example,

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.872
I've never seen a project
where, you know, a law school

00:02:56.872 --> 00:03:01.000
does something where you
grow a plant or take care of

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.328
an animal in order to
learn a principle of law.

00:03:03.328 --> 00:03:06.790
But, we do have some new
interdisciplinary work.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:09.390
And my research center
does some of it already.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.660
So the Information Society
Project does work on

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.000
internet and technology law and policy.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.310
And obviously we work a
lot with public policy,

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:19.785
with history, but a lot with
scientific folks as well,

00:03:19.785 --> 00:03:21.840
because the technology industry

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.520
obviously is very heavily technical.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.508
So we work a lot with
computer science departments,

00:03:27.508 --> 00:03:30.930
with data science departments,
with the eye schools

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.260
that are popping up all
over the nation right now.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.520
There's a lot of interdisciplinary work.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.360
But specifically my work
is about open internet

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.430
and open access.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.900
And when I submitted the
title for my provocation

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.117
for this short talk, I
actually meant to write

00:03:48.117 --> 00:03:51.470
'the power of open' but I typo-ed it to

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:53.270
'the power I'd open'.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:54.930
But I thought about it
for a moment, and actually

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.960
I think it makes sense too
to think of it in terms of

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.580
what sort power would we
try to open up if we could

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.060
as people who have knowledge

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.560
and who try to put out
knowledge in the world.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.471
So for my work, we think
about open internet,

00:04:08.471 --> 00:04:11.535
and threats to open internet
and open speech online.

00:04:11.535 --> 00:04:14.150
So this can include
something like, for example,

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.896
a government completely
shutting down internet access,

00:04:16.896 --> 00:04:20.590
or shutting down or
censoring different websites.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:23.730
As many of you know, for example,
there's a concept of what

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.260
people call The Great Firewall in China,

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.755
which is where many websites
are blocked in China,

00:04:28.755 --> 00:04:32.078
often it's because they
allow for certain news items

00:04:32.078 --> 00:04:33.780
or certain information the government

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.982
doesn't want people to know.

00:04:35.982 --> 00:04:38.534
But not only that, we also
have threats including

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:41.228
laws that may be very well intentioned,

00:04:41.228 --> 00:04:44.542
but may have negative
consequences for open knowledge.

00:04:44.542 --> 00:04:48.820
For example, we have a number
of laws in the EU right now

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:50.560
that are trying to

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.710
set specific rules for
tech companies regarding

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.090
the content that they host online.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.300
Now, traditionally we've
had this history of allowing

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.480
tech companies some immunity.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.054
So if they host content that
may be illegal for some reason,

00:05:04.054 --> 00:05:08.010
they may be immune for legal
consequences due to some laws

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:09.430
that we have in place.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.670
However, these news laws kind
of get rid of that immunity.

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:15.541
And this makes it so that
if there's content that

00:05:15.541 --> 00:05:18.740
is deemed illegal, the company
will have to pay a fine

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:20.620
or face legal consequences.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.330
And the ultimate consequence
of that is that these companies

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:26.510
just tend to over sensor, or block things,

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:29.390
or filter things out before
they even get posted.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:32.155
And that I think is a negative
consequence, and negative

00:05:32.155 --> 00:05:35.660
not just for tech companies
or internet users,

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:38.845
but for the ideas of having
open free speech online

00:05:38.845 --> 00:05:43.170
and having open knowledge and
open access to information.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.890
If nothing else, what
I research is, really

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:48.820
how can we further those goals.

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:53.470
How can we further open
expression, free speech,

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:57.010
and the idea of, the lofty idea
of having democratic values

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.880
that are fueled by people
who have knowledge and

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.740
are able to share knowledge.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.110
So that's specifically
what I'm working on.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.380
But I'm also really
interested in knowing how

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.200
the rest of you and
everyone here is able to

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.010
kind of push those boundaries
forward and bring knowledge

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.240
either to an open space
or to the public or to

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.660
include people who may not normally be

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.810
included in the conversation.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.630
I think those are what
really interests me in

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:22.573
the themes of today's symposium.

00:06:22.573 --> 00:06:25.635
Thank you.

00:06:25.635 --> 00:06:27.885
(applause)

00:06:30.985 --> 00:06:31.990
- Thank you.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.824
Our next speaker we'd
like to introduce is,

00:06:33.824 --> 00:06:36.510
are Alice Kaplan and Christophe Schuwey

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:37.961
from the French department.

00:06:37.961 --> 00:06:40.500
They'll be presenting on
mapping post-war networks

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.653
in an index for combat from 1944 to 1947.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:56.000
- Alice Kaplan will join us eventually,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.940
she has been delayed by

00:07:00.821 --> 00:07:02.823
her task from being a Chair.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.370
So, our...

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.400
So, I think it's quite
timely to have a provocative

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.900
round table here about
digital humanities considering

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:17.950
what's going on in the chronicles of

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.970
higher education right now.

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:22.740
So, whether it is a network
map, a digital edition,

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.100
or a database, digital
humanities are meaningful when

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.450
they renew the way we
perceive our objects.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:32.720
And this is what we are trying
to do in the Combat Project,

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.020
use computer to analyze a
famous newspaper that is

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.203
simply too big for one brain.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:43.080
The goal is to challenge
received ideas about networks

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.460
and newspapers in post-war France.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:50.180
So exploring combat, we are
focusing on signed and unsigned

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:52.450
articles published in the
French daily newspaper

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:55.960
Combat from 1944 to 1947.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:58.110
So that's four years worth of data.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.000
This is a first step in a larger project

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.150
called Mapping Post-War Networks.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:05.653
So what was Combat and why
is it worth mapping, briefly.

00:08:05.653 --> 00:08:08.142
Combat was the most
innovative and most admired

00:08:08.142 --> 00:08:11.719
daily newspaper in post-war France.

00:08:11.719 --> 00:08:15.100
It begins as an underground
resistance newspaper

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:16.120
and a deliberation.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:18.620
The editors, Albert Camus and Pascal Pia,

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:21.890
assembled a group of young,
forward thinking writers

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:24.150
and intellectuals who reported nationally

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.990
and internationally on
politics and culture.

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:29.950
The paper nourished the careers
of many important writers

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:33.660
and thinkers and set a high
standard among all the post-war

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:37.023
newspapers for its style
and intellectual ambitions.

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.620
So because of the very
form of this media and

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:43.656
the complexity of the epoch,
historians of the post-war era

00:08:43.656 --> 00:08:47.640
mainly studied individual
intellectual trajectories.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.717
The individual careers of
Camus, of Sartre, Beauvoir,

00:08:50.717 --> 00:08:53.870
have been tracked down
on a nearly daily basis,

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:57.430
while group biographies
such as Sarah Bakewell,

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:00.253
like the Existentialist Cafe, have woven

00:09:00.253 --> 00:09:03.440
their intellectual lives together.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:06.870
But because of the way this
is shaped, everything was done

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:10.321
on a kind of one to one history, right?

00:09:10.321 --> 00:09:13.494
Our Combat index gives us
the opportunity to look at

00:09:13.494 --> 00:09:16.523
the same period from
a radically new angle.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.534
Rather than focusing on
star individuals across

00:09:20.534 --> 00:09:24.660
the issues of combat, we
explored a network of writers

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:28.333
to understand who wrote for
Combat and how the newspaper

00:09:28.333 --> 00:09:31.560
evolved over time in order
to get a different picture

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.850
of the post-war France network.

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:36.190
Post-war France is a
period preoccupied with

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:40.830
two ideological models, communism
and American capitalism,

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:44.330
and it is not all clear how
Combat navigated these waters.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:47.411
When it veered left, when right.

00:09:47.411 --> 00:09:50.283
In particular there is
a lot of debate about

00:09:50.283 --> 00:09:53.040
to what extent Combat was
influenced by de Gaulle

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.010
the leading figure of the
resistance and some historians

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:58.230
think it became Gaullist very quickly,

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.890
and others insist it resisted.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:03.010
By exploring the newspaper
through a network map,

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:04.900
we would like to reshape this idea

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:07.443
by better understanding
the dynamics going on.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.810
So the project started with
PDFs, and thanks to support from

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:15.040
Yale's Digital Humanities
Lab, we are working with a

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.570
searchable PDF of a full
collection of Combat

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:19.850
obtained from the French National Library.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:23.260
And already working with
a full-text searchable PDF

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:25.860
is already doing digital
humanities and is already

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.020
changing the way we can work
with this source, right?

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:32.330
So from this base, we begin
to build an index manually.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:34.890
It includes categories that
are objective enough to

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.930
be relevant for a network
graph, and reach enough

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:41.370
to allow us to analyze
the data from many angles.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.010
So, here you can find
some of the categories

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:45.653
we are indexing.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.470
So the technology decides
on what we are doing, right?

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:51.170
The question we are asking and our

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:54.812
perspective on our object, for
the better and for the worst.

00:10:54.812 --> 00:10:56.804
Two challenge, for instance.

00:10:56.804 --> 00:11:01.120
The first challenge is that
obviously a spreadsheet,

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:05.888
like this, does violence
to historical complexity.

00:11:05.888 --> 00:11:08.261
It may seem relatively easy to determine

00:11:08.261 --> 00:11:10.983
the nationality of writer,
but let's consider the case of

00:11:10.983 --> 00:11:13.402
Jean Malaquais, for instance.

00:11:13.402 --> 00:11:16.730
Malaquais, born in Poland,
stateless in France,

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:19.910
finally naturalized
American, write in French,

00:11:19.910 --> 00:11:21.700
and won a French literary prize.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:23.637
He publishes an article
in Combat explaining

00:11:23.637 --> 00:11:26.010
American literature to the French.

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.660
And it's even harder to generalize about

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:31.007
the political affinity of
the author in a period where

00:11:31.007 --> 00:11:34.008
the wrong politics could
send you to a firing squad.

00:11:34.008 --> 00:11:36.870
And where the political
affinities which so often between

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:41.870
1944 and 1946, there were
writers who slide from fascism

00:11:42.247 --> 00:11:44.993
to communism to anti-communism.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:48.620
So, what we are trying
to do in these in cases

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:50.620
is to think from the end.

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:53.300
To consider the goal of our analysis.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:56.677
What set of questions do we
want to ask to this data?

00:11:56.677 --> 00:12:00.778
Would it be more relevant to
categorize Malaquais as Polish,

00:12:00.778 --> 00:12:03.220
French, or American depending on that?

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:06.073
Well, Malaquais returning
to France, describing Combat

00:12:06.073 --> 00:12:10.200
as a French writer with an
expertise in American literature

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:14.420
will be categorized, sorry, as French.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:17.910
Another challenge is
obviously collecting the data,

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:21.440
and in our case the more data
we have, good data at least,

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:23.580
the more patterns we can see.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:27.240
And the data entry has
been slow, heroic even,

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.850
and we are really grateful
to our collaborators,

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:33.447
Robin Pront, Anita Sosseuir,
Rafael Vegon, Walid Bushakur,

00:12:33.447 --> 00:12:34.970
for their contribution.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:37.660
But in the next phase of
the project we had to find

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:41.370
more money to be able to
digitize, to hire a company

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:44.580
to digitize nearly 3000 pages of Combat,

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:48.947
so that's more than 50,000
articles that will be

00:12:48.947 --> 00:12:51.023
then available for analysis.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:54.040
We will then be able to see this period

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:55.670
from a whole new perspective.

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:58.328
Who participated in this
newspaper, collaborated,

00:12:58.328 --> 00:13:03.328
and celebrated then and now as
a flagship of French freedom?

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.590
What information did the
Combat journalists gather,

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:09.837
and where did it come from?

00:13:09.837 --> 00:13:13.590
So, I'm saying we will be able to do that.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:15.820
And this has made me my provocative point,

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:18.952
just by digitizing this, just
by building a network map,

00:13:18.952 --> 00:13:22.864
digital mapping will not
provide us with answers,

00:13:22.864 --> 00:13:25.513
because digital humanities never do.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.570
They help answering the question.

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:31.930
The goal of this network
visualization will be to

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:35.775
provide us, the researchers,
with an interface to delve into

00:13:35.775 --> 00:13:37.930
those three years of Combat.

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:40.770
It will allow us to read
those three years of Combat

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:42.360
from a very different angle,

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:45.420
signaling unexpected
connections, phenomenons that

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:48.260
would have never been possible to see into

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:52.440
the text reading Combat in the normal way.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.587
And that will ultimately
invite us to dig into new cases

00:13:55.587 --> 00:13:58.830
with a whole new set of questions.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:01.035
And this is maybe true this interface

00:14:01.035 --> 00:14:02.730
that we can do justice to

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:05.570
an extraordinary period of
the French cultural history

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:08.950
and to make visible this
remark by Simone de Beauvoir

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:12.077
in her post-war memoir, and I quote,

00:14:12.077 --> 00:14:15.771
"To be twenty or twenty-five
in September 0f '44 seemed

00:14:15.771 --> 00:14:20.771
the most fantastic piece of
luck: all roads lay open.

00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:25.290
Journalists, writers, budding
film makers, were all arguing,

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.831
planning, passionately
deciding, as if their future

00:14:27.831 --> 00:14:31.360
depended on no one but themselves."

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:32.569
Thank you very much.

00:14:32.569 --> 00:14:34.819
(applause)

00:14:37.956 --> 00:14:38.960
- Thank you so much.

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.790
For our next speaker we
have Mr. Joseph Zinter,

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.503
from Engineering and
Applied Science and CEID.

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.780
- So the Center for Engineering,
Innovation, and Design,

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:51.790
much like the digital humanities lab,

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:54.226
is open to all of Yale.

00:14:54.226 --> 00:14:58.280
And over the last few years,
we've developed a very

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:02.500
diverse community of people
pursuing a variety of

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:04.740
really, really interesting projects.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:07.020
What I'd like to do today is just show two

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:11.300
very short videos, one demonstrates
a project that was done

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:13.420
in collaboration with
the department of French,

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:16.720
and the second will be
a collaboration with

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:19.833
the Institute for the
Preservation of Cultural Heritage.

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.157
(train horn)

00:15:31.405 --> 00:15:34.863
- I think what I wanted
to teach the students is

00:15:34.863 --> 00:15:38.686
the importance of close reading,
why is it important to do

00:15:38.686 --> 00:15:41.693
close reading in the class
and what does it look

00:15:41.693 --> 00:15:44.030
Yeah to close reading in context.

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:47.660
So, when you have this
train you are forced to

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:49.860
look closely at the text, to produce it.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:54.620
In Zola's text, the train
is a metaphor because Zola

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:58.080
exaggerates the proportion,
but it's also a metaphor

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:01.220
for progress, for history,

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:04.000
for women, because the
train has a woman's name,

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:06.106
for sexuality and desire,

00:16:06.106 --> 00:16:08.830
and also for human bestiality.

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:12.410
I would say they are the three
main metaphor or three main

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:15.210
concepts that the train
symbolizes in this text.

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:18.500
We were looking at descriptions,
in French and in English,

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:21.070
from the text and we were
reading those descriptions

00:16:21.070 --> 00:16:24.410
to the engineers, and they
were trying to, you know,

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:29.350
retranslate what we were saying
into an object, into code.

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:33.850
So you know, really reading
sentences, art, and aiming at

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:35.710
translating into an object.

00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:38.540
And that was really interesting
to me because I remember

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:41.070
Dean Vince was saying that

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:43.590
all this process is a translation.

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:46.480
Zola was translating
the images of trains so

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:51.480
when he was young, then the
text was translated in English

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.651
and then this English
text was translated into

00:16:54.651 --> 00:16:58.673
a computer program that
ended up printing a 3D train.

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:06.320
- Okay, the next example I'd
like to show was one from our

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:10.653
course Introduction to
Engineering Innovation and Design.

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:16.230
(classical music)

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:19.270
- [Narrator] Michelangelo's
David, strong, youthful,

00:17:19.270 --> 00:17:21.850
standing tall throughout the centuries.

00:17:21.850 --> 00:17:24.650
But the standing is becoming a problem.

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:28.650
- Over time David has started
tilting forward, and so that's

00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:31.560
creating hairline cracks
in the back of its ankles.

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:33.960
The sculpture is probably
in a couple centuries

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:35.230
going to fall over.

00:17:35.230 --> 00:17:37.040
- [Narrator] Michelle knows
all about this because

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:40.530
she and her fellow students
built a way to study it

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:43.200
in an introductory engineering class.

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:45.780
- Each group gets assigned
their own project that

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.636
helps someone at Yale with
a problem that they have.

00:17:48.636 --> 00:17:51.100
- [Narrator] Michelle's
group worked with Yale's

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:54.020
Institute for the Preservation
of Cultural Heritage,

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:57.390
know as the IPCH, which
wanted a way to study

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:00.763
what happens to sculptures
like David over time.

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:06.290
The students built this machine,
which they nicknamed Fred,

00:18:06.290 --> 00:18:08.557
and models that mimic
the angle and pressure

00:18:08.557 --> 00:18:10.330
of David's ankles.

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:14.020
- Essentially, our design
created a machine that applied

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:17.030
force downward until the models
that we inserted in between

00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:19.880
these two plates were compressed
enough where they broke.

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:23.014
- This is the time,
this is the total force,

00:18:23.014 --> 00:18:25.490
and we can see it just goes
down as the model cracks.

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:28.850
- [Narrator] David is made of
marble, but the IPCH wants to

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:32.350
study the effects of this
pressure on multiple materials,

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:36.093
so the group also made models
out of concrete and clay.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:39.560
- It was definitely a really
good experience getting to be

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:42.233
hands on so fast, 'cause
it really let me see

00:18:42.233 --> 00:18:45.030
if it was something I wanted to do.

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:47.600
- It's really satisfying
to me, because you see

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:49.620
these students who when they
first showed up they maybe

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:52.010
didn't have any background
in building things and

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:55.640
making things and now they're
doing all this great stuff.

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:57.670
- [Narrator] As for David, well for now,

00:18:57.670 --> 00:18:59.292
he's still standing tall.

00:18:59.292 --> 00:19:02.005
Hopefully, with the help
of research like this,

00:19:02.005 --> 00:19:03.978
he can stay that way.

00:19:03.978 --> 00:19:06.811
(classical music)

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:13.666
- Okay, so just two quick
projects to demonstrate

00:19:13.666 --> 00:19:15.780
the breadth and diversity of

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.500
the type of work that we're doing.

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:20.410
The engineering school
is so proud of its work

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:24.213
with the humanities that
two of its last four

00:19:24.213 --> 00:19:28.200
yearly magazines are proudly

00:19:29.190 --> 00:19:30.740
displaying these projects,

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:31.573
so thank you.

00:19:32.525 --> 00:19:34.775
(applause)

00:19:38.750 --> 00:19:39.610
- Thank you.

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:41.060
For our next speaker we'll have

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:43.020
Peter Leonard from the DHLab.

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.850
He'll be presenting Every
Pixel on the Sunset Strip:

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:48.093
Ed Ruscha Between Archive and Data Set.

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:02.360
- Terrific, so first
I'm really just curious,

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:04.410
how many people in the
room, raise your hand if

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:06.350
you've heard of Ed Ruscha before.

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:08.280
That's great, that's more than most.

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:10.029
We're really excited to talk today about

00:20:10.029 --> 00:20:13.680
a really important pop
artist, a really important

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:15.130
collaboration with the Getty,

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:17.150
and some interesting
work in computer vision.

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:18.520
For those who aren't
familiar with Ed Ruscha,

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:20.292
he comes from Nebraska,
comes to the west coast,

00:20:20.292 --> 00:20:23.296
does all sorts of interesting
artistic work, painting,

00:20:23.296 --> 00:20:26.467
photography, he's most famous
maybe for the artist's book

00:20:26.467 --> 00:20:29.022
Every Building on the
Sunset Strip, which I've put

00:20:29.022 --> 00:20:31.300
two pictures of at the very
top of the screen and at

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:33.880
the bottom Ruscha himself
looking at proofs of the book.

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:35.621
It's an enormous, complicated volume,

00:20:35.621 --> 00:20:38.360
it's small in some dimensions
then when you fold it out

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:41.490
it's really long, and you can
find copies of this at Yale,

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:44.190
at least there's one in the
Haas Art Special Collection,

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:45.470
I think there's one at the Beinecke too,

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:47.440
I really encourage you to look at

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:49.054
Every Building on the Sunset Strip.

00:20:49.054 --> 00:20:52.430
It's an artist's book created
out of an archive that

00:20:52.430 --> 00:20:55.740
Ruscha himself built by putting
a camera in a pickup truck

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:59.690
and driving it down the streets
of Los Angeles in the 1960s.

00:20:59.690 --> 00:21:01.820
It's really kind of an early
form of Google street view,

00:21:01.820 --> 00:21:03.466
in fact Ruscha would
probably claim he invented

00:21:03.466 --> 00:21:05.270
the idea of Google street view.

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:07.430
But this is the 1960s,
it's black and white,

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:10.760
and sort of carefully hand assembles this

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:12.250
in a completely pre-digital era.

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:14.630
It's worth pointing out
that Ruscha has continued

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:15.860
to do this work for decades.

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:18.990
There's still vans driving
around the city of Los Angeles

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.440
manned by his team putting
these things together.

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:26.360
This archive of photographs
that was built into collections

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:28.000
like Every Building on the Sunset Strip

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:29.720
has recently come to the Getty.

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:31.970
And the Getty Research
Institute put out a call for

00:21:31.970 --> 00:21:35.018
researcher projects to
consider how we might think of

00:21:35.018 --> 00:21:38.100
Ruscha and his work, not
just in the context of

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:39.459
the couple dozen images that comprise

00:21:39.459 --> 00:21:42.170
Every Building on the Sunset
Strip, but the hundreds

00:21:42.170 --> 00:21:44.959
of thousands of negatives
that nobody's ever seen

00:21:44.959 --> 00:21:48.740
that go into this long,
linear visualization,

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:53.530
this kind of banal, pop
art, vision of the city.

00:21:53.530 --> 00:21:56.390
And when the lab applied to
be one of the teams that would

00:21:56.390 --> 00:21:59.480
work with collection of hundreds
of thousands of negatives,

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:01.400
we knew that we had a problem on our hands

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:03.530
because Ruscha had already
accomplished perhaps

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:06.430
the most important work
with his photography.

00:22:06.430 --> 00:22:08.860
He had created this really
important artist's book,

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:11.570
and we felt in considering
an archive of hundreds of

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:14.270
thousands of pictures that we
really didn't wanna sort of

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:17.240
replicate or create a robotic
version of the artist's book

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:21.040
which already exists in all
sort of special collections.

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:23.260
So we decided to take a
different path, we wanted to

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:26.520
think about building an
urban heatmap of sort of

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:29.774
geospatial visualization
of discreet objects

00:22:29.774 --> 00:22:31.890
inside the photography.

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:33.360
And what this meant is we had to get into

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:36.140
the world of semantic
segmentation, of masking out

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:38.387
what's actually in each image.

00:22:38.387 --> 00:22:41.304
And so, I'll take you
through our process for this,

00:22:41.304 --> 00:22:43.500
but the basic notion to hear is that

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:45.900
this is a step beyond image captioning.

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:47.960
Our cell phones can already
tell us what pictures

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:50.240
we've taken of cats,
that's just a convolutional

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:52.406
neural network that can
provide image captions.

00:22:52.406 --> 00:22:54.980
Behind the scenes they actually
know what part of the image

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:56.313
that cat is in in your pictures,

00:22:56.313 --> 00:22:58.360
if it's the top or the bottom.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.923
A stage beyond that towards
semantic segmentation

00:23:00.923 --> 00:23:03.160
is an example in the
bottom of this screen.

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:05.090
So we have a really complicated image,

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:07.330
there's all sorts of things going on here,

00:23:07.330 --> 00:23:09.890
but a masking recurrent
neural network has actually

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:12.197
identified the bicycles
as discreet from the boy

00:23:12.197 --> 00:23:15.226
holding out his hand as
discreet from the rider.

00:23:15.226 --> 00:23:18.090
This is done all by sort
of computational work,

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:20.303
there's no humans involved at this phase.

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:22.610
And so what we wanted to do was see

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:25.200
would this technique work
on Ed Ruscha's photography?

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.100
And so this is an actual negative from

00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:29.850
the Getty Research Institute,
taken by Ed Ruscha's team

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:32.140
in the '60s, and you can
see it's the Sunset Strip.

00:23:32.140 --> 00:23:34.266
And the algorithm has expertly identified

00:23:34.266 --> 00:23:36.270
a Volkswagen Beetle.

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:37.940
Now what's important about this is that

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:39.912
its never seen a Volkswagen
Beetle before, right?

00:23:39.912 --> 00:23:42.750
It's been trained on
much more modern cars.

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:44.780
But the convolutional
neural network is capable

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:47.670
of doing this masking, this
semantic segmentation to tell us

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:50.650
exactly where the cars,
and the specific outlines,

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:52.790
not just the bounding boxes.

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:55.730
Now this is actually part of
our idea for this interface,

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:57.960
for building an urban heatmap with really

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:00.110
semantic segmented objects on it.

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:02.838
We've taken the idea of a
car just as our first demo.

00:24:02.838 --> 00:24:05.170
As you can see here, what
this might look like,

00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:08.070
we're looking at a map of Los
Angeles, and we have kind of

00:24:08.070 --> 00:24:09.980
a way of navigating through the city here

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:12.409
and looking where particular items are.

00:24:12.409 --> 00:24:15.074
You'll see that in this case
our demonstration is cars,

00:24:15.074 --> 00:24:17.039
and if you look at the
very bottom of the screen,

00:24:17.039 --> 00:24:19.507
you'll see that we're
actually blurring all of

00:24:19.507 --> 00:24:22.713
the photography and only
things that are crisp

00:24:22.713 --> 00:24:25.465
are the detected cars at the bottom.

00:24:25.465 --> 00:24:29.110
We created this heatmap, or
this sort of visualization

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:33.300
of where automobiles are in the
map, in Rusha's photography,

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:36.340
but we're not trying to
replicate the artist's book.

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:39.502
The interface is inspired by
the aesthetic of this work,

00:24:39.502 --> 00:24:42.470
but we really want this to
be about something else.

00:24:42.470 --> 00:24:44.610
To be about discovering
the discreet objects

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:46.762
within this archive of
hundred of thousands

00:24:46.762 --> 00:24:48.893
of pieces of urban photography.

00:24:50.540 --> 00:24:53.452
In addition to finding discreet objects,

00:24:53.452 --> 00:24:56.190
we wanna be able to nuance
and get more specific

00:24:56.190 --> 00:24:58.290
about what's actually in these objects.

00:24:58.290 --> 00:25:00.730
So one of the things we can
do is we can sort of begin

00:25:00.730 --> 00:25:03.180
the path down the road
towards transfer learning,

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:04.870
or retraining neural networks to be

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:07.240
more specific about what they find.

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:09.790
In this image we've
retrained the final layer of

00:25:09.790 --> 00:25:11.955
a classification network
to tell us whether a car

00:25:11.955 --> 00:25:14.490
is or is not a Volkswagen Beetle.

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:16.770
And you can see we get
really high accuracy on this,

00:25:16.770 --> 00:25:18.410
this isn't sort of cherry picked.

00:25:18.410 --> 00:25:20.358
This is a really great Beetle detector.

00:25:20.358 --> 00:25:22.420
Now that's not actually
the most important thing.

00:25:22.420 --> 00:25:24.640
What we wanna do is we wanna
take what we've learned about

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:28.057
semantic segmentation and the
ability to find objects in

00:25:28.057 --> 00:25:30.340
an archive of hundreds of thousands of

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:32.760
black and white negatives,
and work with folks who

00:25:32.760 --> 00:25:34.627
are really interested in the
urban history of Los Angeles

00:25:34.627 --> 00:25:36.490
and the built environment.

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:39.320
We're interested in training
networks, to not find cars,

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:41.916
but to find perhaps art
deco in glass brick,

00:25:41.916 --> 00:25:46.090
or the red tile of mission
revival architecture,

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:48.010
or maybe even the urban fabric of LA

00:25:48.010 --> 00:25:50.860
in terms of its benches
for buses, its bodegas,

00:25:50.860 --> 00:25:52.730
its laundromats and stores.

00:25:52.730 --> 00:25:55.380
Even the vanishing amount of
single family bungalow houses

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:58.040
that were still present in the 1960s.

00:25:58.040 --> 00:25:59.730
So that's an early glimpse into our

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:01.099
Every Pixel on the Sunset Strip

00:26:01.099 --> 00:26:03.430
research collaboration with the Getty.

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:05.700
We have a brief page of
information about this

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:07.920
on our website, we look forward to putting

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:09.540
more information online soon.

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:11.076
Thanks so much.

00:26:11.076 --> 00:26:13.326
(applause)

00:26:17.857 --> 00:26:19.520
- Thank you very much, and last we have

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:23.143
Philip Corlett and Jane
Garrison presenting to us.

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:40.301
- Hi, my name's Phil Corlett,

00:26:40.301 --> 00:26:44.470
I'm an associate professor
in the Psychiatry department,

00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:46.334
and my work focuses on

00:26:46.334 --> 00:26:50.160
how our brains create the
reality that we perceive,

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:52.100
and I'm particularly
interested in this term here,

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:56.440
psychosis, which is the
departure from contextual reality

00:26:56.440 --> 00:26:58.890
that characterizes some
serious mental illnesses

00:26:58.890 --> 00:26:59.730
like schizophrenia.

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:01.981
So believing things that don't pertain and

00:27:01.981 --> 00:27:04.893
perceiving things that
aren't really there.

00:27:06.320 --> 00:27:08.750
And the way that we understand
the symptoms is as this

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:11.330
sort of trade off for
attention between what

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:15.830
we've learned to expect about
our world through experience,

00:27:15.830 --> 00:27:18.450
perhaps captured in
something like one those

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:21.172
convolutional neural networks
that we just heard about,

00:27:21.172 --> 00:27:23.730
and then applying what we've learned to

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:25.080
what we're currently perceiving.

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:27.385
So as you see this rotating
mask of Charlie Chaplin's face,

00:27:27.385 --> 00:27:30.760
something unusual may have happened.

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:33.960
The mask is rotating in
a clockwise direction and

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:37.260
continues to rotate like that,
however as you start to see

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:40.281
the inside of the face, your
very strong beliefs that

00:27:40.281 --> 00:27:44.037
faces tend to be convex
rather than concave and that

00:27:44.037 --> 00:27:47.440
light falls onto convex
rather than concave objects

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:50.898
in particular ways will drive
you to edit your conclusions

00:27:50.898 --> 00:27:52.730
about what you're seeing.

00:27:52.730 --> 00:27:54.840
And we think that's
what we do all the time

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:56.650
when we're navigating the world.

00:27:56.650 --> 00:27:59.250
We think that people who
hallucinate in their everyday lives

00:27:59.250 --> 00:28:00.720
may do that excessively.

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:01.770
Did you all see that?

00:28:03.130 --> 00:28:04.580
So it's a pretty profound, I think,

00:28:04.580 --> 00:28:09.160
example of how our learning
shapes what we see.

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:12.040
And in my lab we've tried
to sort of think about ways

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:14.742
in which computational
architectures might give rise to

00:28:14.742 --> 00:28:18.320
that learning and how we
might model, if you like,

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:21.300
hallucinations and delusions in silico.

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:24.270
So you can imagine that your
brain is just a series of

00:28:24.270 --> 00:28:27.350
sort of layers of neurons
interacting with one another,

00:28:27.350 --> 00:28:30.690
sending predictions like face
point outwards, downwards,

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:33.750
and data going upwards
through the network.

00:28:33.750 --> 00:28:36.210
And you can think about
sort of the trade offs

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:38.796
between those networks in terms
of predictions that are made

00:28:38.796 --> 00:28:40.550
and prediction errors.

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:43.810
And the neural machinery in
your brain is really helping you

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:45.930
weight which thing to believe.

00:28:45.930 --> 00:28:48.888
In the case of the hollow
mask, you're weighted towards

00:28:48.888 --> 00:28:51.979
your very strong experience
in the world that

00:28:51.979 --> 00:28:54.150
faces tend to point outwards.

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:55.891
And in my lab we try and pick apart

00:28:55.891 --> 00:29:00.160
the neurochemical mechanisms
and neuro structural mechanisms

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:03.967
that give rise to these sorts
of mechanisms in order to

00:29:03.967 --> 00:29:06.850
be able to perhaps better treat people

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:10.106
who suffer from delusions
and hallucinations.

00:29:10.106 --> 00:29:12.100
But interestingly we can think about just

00:29:12.100 --> 00:29:14.090
in a very simple way,
what might come about

00:29:14.090 --> 00:29:15.900
if we perturb these networks.

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:19.130
So at the lowest levels we
might, if we over weighted

00:29:19.130 --> 00:29:21.440
peoples' beliefs at the
lower levels of the network,

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:24.170
induced very simple, basic hallucinations,

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:26.710
the sorts of things that people
experience when they take

00:29:26.710 --> 00:29:29.325
psychedelic, hallucinogenic
drugs for example.

00:29:29.325 --> 00:29:32.155
And at higher doses of those drugs or

00:29:32.155 --> 00:29:35.346
endogenous psychotic illness,
complex hallucinations

00:29:35.346 --> 00:29:38.320
might come about when the
whole network is perturbed.

00:29:38.320 --> 00:29:41.779
And you start to see new
objects cut out of whole cloth

00:29:41.779 --> 00:29:45.493
appearing before our eyes our ears.

00:29:46.503 --> 00:29:48.760
Let me see if I can show you this video.

00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:51.632
So this is an example from
some colleagues at Sussex

00:29:51.632 --> 00:29:56.450
who have taken Google
DeepDream and perturbed

00:29:56.450 --> 00:30:00.090
the lowest levels of its
network, applying it now to

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:02.150
a video of the marketplace in Sussex.

00:30:02.150 --> 00:30:02.983
And you can see these sort of

00:30:02.983 --> 00:30:06.200
subtle geometric changes
that are happening.

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:08.640
So it looks sort of unusual
but not unintelligible,

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:10.690
and not entirely unexpected, it just looks

00:30:10.690 --> 00:30:12.070
sort of low quality.

00:30:12.070 --> 00:30:14.200
And like I said, these
sort of geometric patterns

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:16.991
seem to be the sorts of things
that people experience when

00:30:16.991 --> 00:30:21.623
they take serotonergic hallucinogens
like LSD or psilocybin.

00:30:26.069 --> 00:30:27.319
And just to show you now,

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:29.953
sorry.

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:35.620
What might happen when we make
the same sort of manipulation

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:37.150
to the highest levels of the network

00:30:37.150 --> 00:30:39.170
whilst looking at the same video.

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:41.390
So now, the network has been trained on

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:44.176
lots of different photographs,
and it's inserting objects

00:30:44.176 --> 00:30:46.610
into the video that really aren't there.

00:30:46.610 --> 00:30:49.343
So you can see dogs, puffer fish,

00:30:51.010 --> 00:30:53.753
eyes seem to be fairly commonly observed.

00:30:55.270 --> 00:30:57.160
And so just taking this
sort of very simple

00:30:57.160 --> 00:31:00.150
network architecture, we
can get some insights into

00:31:00.150 --> 00:31:03.290
what might be happening, we
think, in the brains of people

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:05.890
who are on psychedelic drugs
and in the brains of people who

00:31:05.890 --> 00:31:07.693
have psychotic illnesses.

00:31:10.116 --> 00:31:11.590
And that's all well and good,

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:15.460
but there's nothing in there
or built into the network

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:18.817
that's really perceiving
what's sort of unusual

00:31:18.817 --> 00:31:23.060
or uncomfortable about
what you just experienced.

00:31:23.060 --> 00:31:27.040
Why does it feel so uncanny
or uneasy when your brain

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:29.760
edits the hollowed mask

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:33.140
and causes you to see
it switching direction,

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:34.631
and what would it mean if that

00:31:34.631 --> 00:31:37.580
uncanniness or uneasiness
were not present.

00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:40.066
And so with the digital humanities lab

00:31:40.066 --> 00:31:43.090
and also some collaborations
at CCAM, we've been

00:31:43.090 --> 00:31:46.000
thinking about ways in which
one might tether two of these

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:48.140
networks together in what's called

00:31:48.140 --> 00:31:50.980
a generative adversarial network.

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:54.274
And the simple idea here is
that one side of one these

00:31:54.274 --> 00:31:56.590
sort of convolutional neural nets might be

00:31:56.590 --> 00:31:59.475
making suggestions about
what's out there in the world,

00:31:59.475 --> 00:32:03.960
and another side might be
critiquing those suggestions

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:06.490
saying that they feel right
or they don't feel right

00:32:06.490 --> 00:32:09.140
and rejecting them, and
that these two nets may be

00:32:09.140 --> 00:32:11.905
training one another and
ultimately giving rise

00:32:11.905 --> 00:32:15.340
to a sensation that, actually,
oh yes, that feels right,

00:32:15.340 --> 00:32:17.940
or that feels wrong somehow.

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:19.910
And so that's what we've
been trying to explore.

00:32:19.910 --> 00:32:22.440
These sorts of architectures
are currently being used

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:25.097
in the digital humanities
lab for various projects,

00:32:25.097 --> 00:32:27.788
and what we're interested in
doing is partnering with folks

00:32:27.788 --> 00:32:30.590
in digital humanities to
play around with these

00:32:30.590 --> 00:32:33.940
network architectures, break
them, and see the ways in which

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:36.952
they break, see the ways in
which we might bias towards

00:32:36.952 --> 00:32:40.980
generation of spurious
information, and see ways in which

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:44.811
we might disrupt the critic
in order to fool the generator

00:32:44.811 --> 00:32:47.448
into believing what's
out there in the world

00:32:47.448 --> 00:32:51.200
is radically different from reality.

00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:52.127
Thank you.

00:32:52.127 --> 00:32:54.377
(applause)

00:32:58.651 --> 00:33:00.300
- Thank you to all of
the speakers for these

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:03.620
amazing presentations and next
we want to make this more of

00:33:03.620 --> 00:33:07.069
a discussion based, so we are
going to open up the floor

00:33:07.069 --> 00:33:09.747
and if any of you have any questions about

00:33:09.747 --> 00:33:12.047
any of these projects
or questions in general.

00:33:14.620 --> 00:33:17.370
So I do want to have
like a leading question

00:33:17.370 --> 00:33:21.680
just to open up, so all of
the speakers mentioned about

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:23.900
the application of like
data driven message

00:33:23.900 --> 00:33:27.001
and the interdisciplinary
solutions, but what do you all

00:33:27.001 --> 00:33:29.621
think is a good disciplinary
marriage in your

00:33:29.621 --> 00:33:34.286
respective field between
data, art, humanities, law,

00:33:34.286 --> 00:33:37.380
and what could potentially
be a bad marriage

00:33:37.380 --> 00:33:39.613
or interdisciplinary solution?

00:33:45.955 --> 00:33:50.940
- Well I think something that
is incredibly interesting,

00:33:50.940 --> 00:33:53.810
and thank you for your
question, about that is don't go

00:33:53.810 --> 00:33:56.120
in debate again that I
mentioned about in chronicles

00:33:56.120 --> 00:33:59.727
about the fact that digital
humanities are a failure

00:33:59.727 --> 00:34:02.690
because automated computing
calculation doesn't

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:04.500
work always in the arts.

00:34:04.500 --> 00:34:06.120
And I think that the problem is that

00:34:06.120 --> 00:34:10.080
the debate is not shaped

00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:12.210
correctly somehow.

00:34:12.210 --> 00:34:16.350
So a bad application of digital
humanities for literature

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:19.263
would be to expect the
computer to think for us.

00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:24.200
Simply because it doesn't.

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:26.500
I mean automated car driving is not about

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:28.920
a car that is thinking,
it is about a car that is

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:31.730
reacting in every situation the
way it is supposed to react.

00:34:31.730 --> 00:34:33.030
That is very different.

00:34:33.030 --> 00:34:38.030
Since in literature we especially
want to do not over that,

00:34:38.140 --> 00:34:41.670
like not to reproduce but
to say for something else.

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:45.393
And a good application is to
use it, and I think that goes,

00:34:47.220 --> 00:34:50.230
that resonates with all the
talks, fascinating talks,

00:34:50.230 --> 00:34:54.170
that have been given
there, is a new interface,

00:34:54.170 --> 00:34:58.252
like new perception of
what we already know.

00:34:58.252 --> 00:35:00.003
Is it a reality?

00:35:01.480 --> 00:35:05.050
Giving access to people
by presenting something

00:35:05.050 --> 00:35:07.020
in a different way than it is normally,

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:11.131
so opening the the access,
or to turn a book into

00:35:11.131 --> 00:35:14.793
suddenly something else,
highlighting something else

00:35:14.793 --> 00:35:19.793
in order either to communicate on this

00:35:19.970 --> 00:35:24.520
or to give us new clues on new
ways to approach our objects,

00:35:24.520 --> 00:35:28.390
so the question makes the
digital humanities relevant

00:35:28.390 --> 00:35:29.493
or not, I would say.

00:35:32.155 --> 00:35:34.520
- Sure, I'd like to add I think.

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:36.378
I see a lot of great
interdisciplinary work,

00:35:36.378 --> 00:35:39.506
I see a lot of legal
research that's being done

00:35:39.506 --> 00:35:41.644
in conjunction with other fields.

00:35:41.644 --> 00:35:45.390
For example, we have
a cybersecurity course

00:35:45.390 --> 00:35:49.040
at the law school, which is
almost purely cybersecurity

00:35:49.040 --> 00:35:53.160
with very little law, because
if you're trying to make laws

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:55.950
about cybersecurity, maybe you should know

00:35:55.950 --> 00:35:58.400
what cybersecurity actually is, right?

00:35:58.400 --> 00:36:00.740
Like that's just something
that we lack a little bit

00:36:00.740 --> 00:36:03.800
in law making, so I
think, if I think of maybe

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:08.270
a bad interdisciplinary
project, it would really just be

00:36:08.270 --> 00:36:10.446
the lack of it in law and policy.

00:36:10.446 --> 00:36:13.680
So you get a lot of laws and
you get a lot of policymakers

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:15.290
who don't understand technology,

00:36:15.290 --> 00:36:18.037
or don't understand science,
or don't believe in science,

00:36:18.037 --> 00:36:21.140
and this results in bad policy.

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:22.560
So that's a big problem.

00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:26.380
And I'm glad that at least
in universities we're working

00:36:26.380 --> 00:36:29.350
in interdisciplinary work
and hopefully we'll push that

00:36:29.350 --> 00:36:31.350
forward as well, not just for government

00:36:31.350 --> 00:36:32.873
but for industry too.

00:36:35.910 --> 00:36:40.430
- So I think an example of
a bad collaboration would be

00:36:40.430 --> 00:36:42.820
sort of falling between
two stools and not honoring

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:45.050
the sort of important
qualities of both sides

00:36:45.050 --> 00:36:48.331
of an interdisciplinary project.

00:36:48.331 --> 00:36:50.493
Sort of not being the best scientist

00:36:50.493 --> 00:36:53.570
and not being the best artist.

00:36:53.570 --> 00:36:56.970
In my own field, I think one
of the fears is if we try

00:36:56.970 --> 00:36:59.105
and think about mental
symptoms in this way,

00:36:59.105 --> 00:37:01.990
we rob them of their deep and important

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:06.100
sort of heritage and meaning for people,

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:09.380
and so I'd hate to see any approach

00:37:09.380 --> 00:37:11.960
that we took to digitize the humanities

00:37:11.960 --> 00:37:15.583
to forget that these sorts of
experiences, these artifacts,

00:37:15.583 --> 00:37:19.084
these pieces of work are
beautiful in their own right,

00:37:19.084 --> 00:37:22.370
and what I would hope is that
any interdisciplinary work

00:37:22.370 --> 00:37:24.870
would enhance that rather
than detracting from it.

00:37:31.060 --> 00:37:33.993
- Are there any audience
questions at this time?

00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:37.430
Okay I can ask another.

00:37:38.560 --> 00:37:40.790
So, when you first...

00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:44.810
What inspired you to get into
this interdisciplinary field

00:37:44.810 --> 00:37:47.583
and what inspired your, like,

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:53.103
introduction of digital methods
to your field of interest?

00:37:57.820 --> 00:38:00.130
- Well I can say that I was inspired by

00:38:00.130 --> 00:38:03.270
the digital humanities
staff, by Peter Leonard,

00:38:03.270 --> 00:38:07.185
I was preparing a colloquium
on post-war magazines,

00:38:07.185 --> 00:38:10.386
post-war cultural magazines and

00:38:10.386 --> 00:38:12.980
we just started playing around.

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:16.210
And I didn't know anything
about the digital humanities

00:38:16.210 --> 00:38:20.200
except having read Franco
Moretti's book on distant reading.

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:23.130
So I had some kind of
intuition about what what

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:27.136
we might be able to do that
an individual researcher

00:38:27.136 --> 00:38:31.346
couldn't do just going through
the pages on a microfilm.

00:38:31.346 --> 00:38:33.110
I mean, like everyone else I had

00:38:33.110 --> 00:38:36.373
the nausea effect of reading microfilm.

00:38:37.430 --> 00:38:39.960
But I think some of us are
doing digital humanities

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:44.960
without knowing it, we do
look inside to find a passage

00:38:45.260 --> 00:38:48.021
when we're preparing a class on a book,

00:38:48.021 --> 00:38:51.062
or we look at Google
Books, I mean it's already,

00:38:51.062 --> 00:38:55.610
it's already there all around
us and what's great about

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:58.760
digital humanities is getting the culture

00:38:58.760 --> 00:39:03.493
to formalize some of
these instinctive moods.

00:39:09.700 --> 00:39:12.550
- So I actually have, part
of the reason why I started

00:39:12.550 --> 00:39:16.082
working in interdisciplinary
sort of digital slash law

00:39:16.082 --> 00:39:18.750
issues is because that's just what

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:20.545
I started doing during law school.

00:39:20.545 --> 00:39:23.890
I'm right now leading an
initiative that's funded in part

00:39:23.890 --> 00:39:26.300
by the Wikimedia foundation,
which is a nonprofit that's

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:28.650
run by Wikipedia, and I'm sure,

00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:30.572
how many of you have used Wikipedia?

00:39:30.572 --> 00:39:32.593
Pretty much everybody.

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:37.690
And I think that having
used those tools through my

00:39:37.690 --> 00:39:40.850
education really led me
to want to work on them

00:39:40.850 --> 00:39:42.390
and work on creating a world where

00:39:42.390 --> 00:39:44.170
we could support those things.

00:39:44.170 --> 00:39:45.940
So one thing I'd be really
interested in knowing

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:48.533
from all of you actually if
you don't mind me asking,

00:39:48.533 --> 00:39:53.533
one of the topics that I work
is the idea of open access.

00:39:54.260 --> 00:39:56.560
And I know for all us
open access scholarship

00:39:56.560 --> 00:39:57.979
is a controversial issue.

00:39:57.979 --> 00:40:01.710
Now how can we either get
our research out there

00:40:01.710 --> 00:40:04.147
for the public, either
through open access journals

00:40:04.147 --> 00:40:06.530
or through public scholarship,

00:40:06.530 --> 00:40:09.015
whether or not you believe
public scholarship exists,

00:40:09.015 --> 00:40:10.907
or other forms like that.

00:40:10.907 --> 00:40:13.660
I'd be really interested in
hearing what you all think

00:40:13.660 --> 00:40:16.170
about that and how we can kind
of move forward with bringing

00:40:16.170 --> 00:40:18.707
a lot of the great
research at this university

00:40:18.707 --> 00:40:20.373
out there to the public.

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:27.833
- I'll try and respond.

00:40:27.833 --> 00:40:32.170
So, I agree it's absolutely sort of

00:40:33.217 --> 00:40:36.873
essential that we make what
we work on and what we produce

00:40:36.873 --> 00:40:39.850
openly available to the public.

00:40:39.850 --> 00:40:42.240
I think what needs to change
in order to allow that

00:40:42.240 --> 00:40:46.850
to happen is either for
journals to make that easier

00:40:47.870 --> 00:40:51.460
or for our funding bodies
and institutions to

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:53.610
give us resources to make that happen.

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:56.570
And I don't see either of those
things happening immediately

00:40:56.570 --> 00:40:58.391
without us being more vocal about

00:40:58.391 --> 00:41:00.233
the fact that that's what we want.

00:41:01.210 --> 00:41:02.260
When I write grants to

00:41:02.260 --> 00:41:04.460
the National Institute of
Mental Health, for example,

00:41:04.460 --> 00:41:06.510
I now build into the budgets and funding

00:41:06.510 --> 00:41:08.940
in order to publish my work open access.

00:41:08.940 --> 00:41:11.530
It's a different model in
the UK, you can't get funding

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:14.100
from the Welcome Trust or
the Medical Research Council

00:41:14.100 --> 00:41:16.820
without committing to
publishing everything

00:41:16.820 --> 00:41:18.320
that you have open access.

00:41:18.320 --> 00:41:20.374
And they're much more further along

00:41:20.374 --> 00:41:23.363
than we are in that regard.

00:41:24.380 --> 00:41:26.540
So I think, you know, having
conversations like this

00:41:26.540 --> 00:41:29.322
with people like yourself and
raising the profile of that

00:41:29.322 --> 00:41:33.147
sort of issue is super important,
and being committed to it

00:41:33.147 --> 00:41:35.803
and enacting that commitment
as much as possible.

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:44.040
- Yeah, maybe I would totally
agree, and I would add also

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:47.520
the way publications
basically are evaluated.

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:49.974
I'm thinking here for instance
of the difference between

00:41:49.974 --> 00:41:53.009
publishing a critical
edition of a work in a book

00:41:53.009 --> 00:41:55.530
with a publisher or online.

00:41:55.530 --> 00:41:57.429
This is absolutely not the same weight

00:41:57.429 --> 00:42:00.460
for a committee, for a resume.

00:42:00.460 --> 00:42:05.110
So maybe there is also
things that could be done to

00:42:07.120 --> 00:42:09.290
encourage the

00:42:09.290 --> 00:42:10.493
open access publication.

00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:14.040
Of course open access
doesn't mean necessarily

00:42:14.040 --> 00:42:17.040
that it's not a journal, but
there is something there still.

00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:21.160
- [Divek] Thanks for the answers there.

00:42:21.160 --> 00:42:23.116
At this point...

00:42:23.116 --> 00:42:24.353
Yes, thank you.

00:42:25.845 --> 00:42:28.210
- Something I think is
connected to these things

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:31.193
you've been talking about in a
big way to the title even is,

00:42:31.193 --> 00:42:33.224
so what happens later?

00:42:33.224 --> 00:42:36.170
What are the ways to think about

00:42:36.170 --> 00:42:39.470
unintended consequences of this research?

00:42:39.470 --> 00:42:43.806
And unacknowledged fore
bearers of this research,

00:42:43.806 --> 00:42:46.310
the surveillance state
and things like that.

00:42:46.310 --> 00:42:48.860
How do these things come
into play in y'all's work?

00:42:56.060 --> 00:42:57.210
- Can you elaborate what you mean by

00:42:57.210 --> 00:42:59.533
the unacknowledged fore
bearers of the work?

00:43:01.100 --> 00:43:05.240
- A lot of the, some
of the work comes from

00:43:06.110 --> 00:43:08.650
entities that have used the algorithms

00:43:08.650 --> 00:43:13.330
to detect political activity,

00:43:13.330 --> 00:43:18.080
to target ads in biased ways,

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:19.163
things like that.

00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:24.790
One argument could be that
using these in humanistic ways

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:27.283
is making a detour of those algorithms,

00:43:30.130 --> 00:43:32.650
I'm sort of playing around in that area.

00:43:32.650 --> 00:43:33.920
- I think it's a fascinating question

00:43:33.920 --> 00:43:37.190
and I don't think there any
sort of straightforward answers.

00:43:37.190 --> 00:43:40.080
I think the relationship between
something like psychology

00:43:40.080 --> 00:43:43.720
and motivational decision
making and, you know,

00:43:43.720 --> 00:43:46.780
entities like Google,
and Twitter, and Facebook

00:43:46.780 --> 00:43:50.683
is tense to say the least.

00:43:52.210 --> 00:43:55.411
I think the only way
that we can combat that

00:43:55.411 --> 00:44:00.030
as academics and practitioners is to

00:44:00.030 --> 00:44:02.940
highlight what's happening
and to make these mechanisms

00:44:02.940 --> 00:44:07.820
as understandable and transparent

00:44:07.820 --> 00:44:09.760
for the public as possible.

00:44:09.760 --> 00:44:12.530
I don't think it would help
anyone if we turned our backs

00:44:12.530 --> 00:44:14.593
on this and didn't study it more deeply.

00:44:15.590 --> 00:44:18.770
That's certainly how I try and
handle things with my work,

00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:20.810
which clearly has also sorts of

00:44:20.810 --> 00:44:22.973
potential nefarious implications.

00:44:26.825 --> 00:44:28.570
- I think that's really
a interesting question.

00:44:28.570 --> 00:44:30.588
Some of my research has
been on privacy law,

00:44:30.588 --> 00:44:33.020
and it gets into these issues.

00:44:33.020 --> 00:44:35.042
So I have two quick things.

00:44:35.042 --> 00:44:39.750
There's one project called the
All Of Us Program by the NIH

00:44:39.750 --> 00:44:43.321
which seeks to collect the DNA
data of a million Americans

00:44:43.321 --> 00:44:46.960
and use that for a public
database that can then, you know,

00:44:46.960 --> 00:44:49.840
ideally generate great medical advances.

00:44:49.840 --> 00:44:51.790
So this is an interesting
example, because here

00:44:51.790 --> 00:44:55.080
you have clearly have a well
intentioned project that could

00:44:55.080 --> 00:44:57.919
lead to breakthroughs in
precision medicine and more,

00:44:57.919 --> 00:45:01.270
but the problem then is that
people don't really know

00:45:01.270 --> 00:45:03.403
what's happening with their DNA data.

00:45:04.410 --> 00:45:07.293
What does it mean when you
give your data to an entity

00:45:07.293 --> 00:45:11.195
that then owns the data and
can do with it what it wants?

00:45:11.195 --> 00:45:14.088
And there's a line in
legal scholarship about,

00:45:14.088 --> 00:45:19.088
in law and medicine, about
consent to use of DNA samples

00:45:19.790 --> 00:45:22.480
or blood samples in medical research.

00:45:22.480 --> 00:45:26.080
For example, there's the
issue of whether or not

00:45:26.080 --> 00:45:28.400
you have any ownership
stake in pharmaceuticals

00:45:28.400 --> 00:45:31.700
that were developed
using research that had

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:34.060
used samples from certain people, right?

00:45:34.060 --> 00:45:35.053
So there are these questions I think

00:45:35.053 --> 00:45:37.170
that we have to deal with.

00:45:37.170 --> 00:45:39.440
But more broadly speaking,

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:41.280
if you're just thinking about privacy,

00:45:41.280 --> 00:45:43.980
I think there are a lot of
interesting techniques done

00:45:43.980 --> 00:45:47.279
right now to protect privacy
so that data can be used

00:45:47.279 --> 00:45:49.555
in these large data science projects.

00:45:49.555 --> 00:45:54.160
I was recently at a privacy
security research event

00:45:54.160 --> 00:45:57.127
at Google, which is one
company that people are

00:45:57.127 --> 00:46:00.880
concerned about, obviously,
for privacy reasons.

00:46:00.880 --> 00:46:03.270
But they have been

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:05.004
just really hiring a ton of

00:46:05.004 --> 00:46:09.050
great academics for better or worse,

00:46:09.050 --> 00:46:12.190
they're taking a lot of
great academics into industry

00:46:12.190 --> 00:46:14.859
to work specifically on
privacy and security,

00:46:14.859 --> 00:46:19.550
de-identifying data, how
to use federated learning

00:46:19.550 --> 00:46:22.558
and other techniques to use the data

00:46:22.558 --> 00:46:25.175
to find results without actually

00:46:25.175 --> 00:46:27.630
being able to re-identify the data,

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:31.710
or being able to connect the
data to the data originator.

00:46:31.710 --> 00:46:32.910
So there's work being done,

00:46:32.910 --> 00:46:35.056
and a lot of it is interdisciplinary.

00:46:35.056 --> 00:46:38.470
Again, with this sort of theme,
I think there is a lot that

00:46:38.470 --> 00:46:41.873
could be done to help alleviate
what you're talking about.

00:46:45.166 --> 00:46:46.164
- Thank for the question.

00:46:46.164 --> 00:46:48.410
Are there, we can take about
two or three more questions,

00:46:48.410 --> 00:46:50.310
does anyone have anything else to say?

00:46:53.190 --> 00:46:55.370
Okay, yeah.

00:46:55.370 --> 00:46:57.130
- I just have a follow up question

00:46:57.130 --> 00:46:58.880
on what we mentioned already about.

00:46:59.870 --> 00:47:04.300
So, so I think that one of the main,

00:47:04.300 --> 00:47:07.520
some of the main purpose that
why we want to digitize things

00:47:07.520 --> 00:47:10.490
or we want to visualize
things is either to

00:47:10.490 --> 00:47:14.270
help researchers better on making some of

00:47:14.270 --> 00:47:17.130
these breakthroughs on
the research, but also

00:47:17.130 --> 00:47:19.470
on the other hand being able to present to

00:47:19.470 --> 00:47:21.710
the general public,
making people understand

00:47:22.935 --> 00:47:26.390
often some really complicated
scientific project.

00:47:26.390 --> 00:47:29.940
But sometimes this is really a
balance, because for example,

00:47:29.940 --> 00:47:32.330
like I grew up reading
Scientific American,

00:47:32.330 --> 00:47:35.710
but then it presents certain
information in a way that

00:47:35.710 --> 00:47:39.570
seems interesting but does
not necessarily follow

00:47:39.570 --> 00:47:43.700
in the exact, like the
scientific rigorous process.

00:47:43.700 --> 00:47:45.733
I'm wondering for many
of the researchers here,

00:47:45.733 --> 00:47:48.439
how do you view that problem on digitizing

00:47:48.439 --> 00:47:51.310
or visualizing some of
these like information,

00:47:51.310 --> 00:47:52.990
because some of the critical information

00:47:52.990 --> 00:47:55.333
could also lost in the
digitization process.

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:07.380
- Thank you for this terrific
question, I think, again,

00:48:07.380 --> 00:48:09.920
sorry to get back to that, but yes,

00:48:09.920 --> 00:48:12.880
that's one of the big thing
that digitizing is bringing

00:48:12.880 --> 00:48:17.880
and is one of the least
discussed thing in a bizarre way.

00:48:18.170 --> 00:48:20.018
We're on a screen so we can do things

00:48:20.018 --> 00:48:22.510
that we couldn't do on paper.

00:48:22.510 --> 00:48:26.536
And because probably of
some cultural background,

00:48:26.536 --> 00:48:30.760
the visual is kind of
the, yeah, we'll see,

00:48:30.760 --> 00:48:33.090
we'll figure out in the
end how we will show this,

00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:35.281
the important is what
is behind, and in fact,

00:48:35.281 --> 00:48:39.030
getting on the screen and
being able to do all the things

00:48:39.030 --> 00:48:41.650
we can do with the screen and
showing things differently,

00:48:41.650 --> 00:48:44.003
I think is one of the major assets of

00:48:44.003 --> 00:48:47.743
any digitalization of any kind.

00:48:49.090 --> 00:48:52.970
- So I work in brain
imaging, where we produce

00:48:54.540 --> 00:48:57.510
maplike representations
of statistical tests

00:48:57.510 --> 00:48:58.740
that make it appear as though

00:48:58.740 --> 00:49:01.350
parts of the brain are lighting up

00:49:01.350 --> 00:49:04.201
or are engaged by particular processes.

00:49:04.201 --> 00:49:06.570
And there's been whole series of papers

00:49:06.570 --> 00:49:09.011
almost cottage industries
of papers being exchanged,

00:49:09.011 --> 00:49:11.206
the seductive allure of brain images,

00:49:11.206 --> 00:49:14.750
the seductive allure
of seductive allure by

00:49:14.750 --> 00:49:17.553
the people who don't
agree, and I think, again,

00:49:17.553 --> 00:49:22.037
it gets down to being clear communicators.

00:49:22.037 --> 00:49:25.185
Telling people that these aren't maps of

00:49:25.185 --> 00:49:30.050
recorded neural activity, but
very circuitously generated,

00:49:30.050 --> 00:49:31.963
statistically massaged,

00:49:33.460 --> 00:49:36.440
visually doctored images

00:49:36.440 --> 00:49:39.410
that represent statistical
tests nevertheless,

00:49:39.410 --> 00:49:41.920
but helping people become
better consumers will

00:49:41.920 --> 00:49:45.400
be more and more important
I think, as we go forward.

00:49:51.500 --> 00:49:53.520
- So, we are seeking for one last question

00:49:53.520 --> 00:49:54.720
from the audience maybe.

00:49:55.940 --> 00:49:58.281
If anyone has, yes.

00:49:58.281 --> 00:50:03.281
- This sort of gets back
to Peter's presentation,

00:50:04.340 --> 00:50:06.000
but also I think many of them.

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:10.060
If you're thinking about
the visual in terms of

00:50:10.060 --> 00:50:12.960
what we are now able to
render and what we will

00:50:12.960 --> 00:50:16.400
be able to render in the
future, is that you're really

00:50:16.400 --> 00:50:20.150
looking at something that
is completely nonlinear.

00:50:20.150 --> 00:50:21.655
I'm an art historian and I'm also

00:50:21.655 --> 00:50:23.650
an arts information manager,

00:50:23.650 --> 00:50:26.490
or have been at a various
points in my life.

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:29.670
And one of the things that
has been deviling to me

00:50:29.670 --> 00:50:33.673
aside from copywriting,
another symposium topic,

00:50:33.673 --> 00:50:38.094
has been the fact that we often
come into the digital realm

00:50:38.094 --> 00:50:42.913
prejudiced essentially by vocabulary.

00:50:44.536 --> 00:50:47.600
And the ability to be able
to break out of that mold,

00:50:47.600 --> 00:50:50.630
I think is truly transformational.

00:50:50.630 --> 00:50:54.040
So the idea of neural
maps or nonlinearity,

00:50:54.040 --> 00:50:57.050
any way that we can express the visual

00:50:57.050 --> 00:50:59.593
without having to sort of predicate it

00:50:59.593 --> 00:51:04.300
by calling it something,
that is expressed in words,

00:51:04.300 --> 00:51:07.930
I think is really something
that we need to see

00:51:07.930 --> 00:51:11.620
as very special, and I
would say is one of the

00:51:11.620 --> 00:51:15.532
counterarguments I would
make to whatever was going on

00:51:15.532 --> 00:51:18.180
from higher ed, which I would have to say

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:21.247
is one of the dumbest
things I've ever heard.

00:51:21.247 --> 00:51:22.810
- That's a great comment.

00:51:22.810 --> 00:51:26.040
I think that one of the things
that we're doing in the lab

00:51:26.040 --> 00:51:29.460
in terms of writing reusable
software is to try to allow

00:51:29.460 --> 00:51:32.755
people to work with cultural
heritage objects without

00:51:32.755 --> 00:51:36.750
necessarily being beholden
to their textual description.

00:51:36.750 --> 00:51:38.730
In the libraries in the the GLAM sector,

00:51:38.730 --> 00:51:42.600
galleries, librarians,
libraries, and museums, archives,

00:51:42.600 --> 00:51:45.540
we oftentimes really
prioritize, for good reason,

00:51:45.540 --> 00:51:47.630
the textual description
of visual cultural.

00:51:47.630 --> 00:51:49.480
We need to have an expert
look at this and determine

00:51:49.480 --> 00:51:51.500
who the painter was and
where it was provenanced,

00:51:51.500 --> 00:51:52.940
that's incredibly important work.

00:51:52.940 --> 00:51:54.664
The Getty does this, YCBA does this.

00:51:54.664 --> 00:51:58.330
But there are also new ways
of measuring visual similarity

00:51:58.330 --> 00:52:01.598
or arranging collections at
the scale of 100,000 objects.

00:52:01.598 --> 00:52:05.080
These purely on their kind
of the analysis of the

00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:08.860
raster pixels, how they
look, and we, I think,

00:52:08.860 --> 00:52:11.300
one of the things that's most
exciting to me in terms of

00:52:11.300 --> 00:52:13.379
machine vision, in terms
of how computers see,

00:52:13.379 --> 00:52:17.120
is the opportunity not to break
away from human description,

00:52:17.120 --> 00:52:19.670
because human description
has within it decades

00:52:19.670 --> 00:52:22.254
and centuries really of
expert provenance and

00:52:22.254 --> 00:52:24.969
sort of connoisseurship,
but to put that human

00:52:24.969 --> 00:52:29.177
textual description in conversation
with a kind of analysis

00:52:29.177 --> 00:52:32.414
of the pixels themselves,
I think a lot of the work,

00:52:32.414 --> 00:52:35.400
if those in the room have
seen the work of the lab

00:52:35.400 --> 00:52:37.209
and Doug Duhaime in terms of PixPlot

00:52:37.209 --> 00:52:38.990
and Neural Neighbors,
that's really exciting

00:52:38.990 --> 00:52:41.693
open source software that
we hope to see deployed

00:52:41.693 --> 00:52:44.280
in even more cultural institutions as an

00:52:44.280 --> 00:52:47.350
alternate way of considering
the visual without having to

00:52:47.350 --> 00:52:49.980
do something like look for
the word horse and miss

00:52:49.980 --> 00:52:52.280
an image because it's
described as equestrian.

00:52:58.640 --> 00:53:01.860
- Maybe if we could have more
questions from the audience,

00:53:01.860 --> 00:53:02.973
if there is any?

00:53:04.240 --> 00:53:05.270
Yes.

00:53:05.270 --> 00:53:08.748
- So, I wanna ask, so there's
the digital and humanities

00:53:08.748 --> 00:53:13.748
and when I think of those
two it reminds me of, like,

00:53:15.040 --> 00:53:17.391
digital, like, you're
looking at sound wave

00:53:17.391 --> 00:53:21.456
and how that's transferred
from a symphonic,

00:53:21.456 --> 00:53:24.803
smooth current wave into a digitalized,

00:53:25.910 --> 00:53:30.710
like, way that kind of cuts
out some of the nuances.

00:53:30.710 --> 00:53:34.580
What do you think can be
lost possibly and how can you

00:53:34.580 --> 00:53:37.400
protect that in the
transition in the marriage of

00:53:37.400 --> 00:53:40.700
the synthesis between like the
digital and the humanities,

00:53:40.700 --> 00:53:45.490
because as we were talking
about how the expert kind of is

00:53:45.490 --> 00:53:49.750
ruling the kingdom here, and the expert is

00:53:49.750 --> 00:53:53.623
traditionally kind of a
western white guy, and so

00:53:53.623 --> 00:53:56.000
the language that kind of comes out of

00:53:56.000 --> 00:53:58.645
that kind of expertise may overlook

00:53:58.645 --> 00:54:02.201
the native capacity and the native morals,

00:54:02.201 --> 00:54:05.890
so how might digital humanities benefit

00:54:05.890 --> 00:54:07.500
from kind of expanding

00:54:09.280 --> 00:54:10.747
the view of what an expert is,

00:54:10.747 --> 00:54:14.800
and this was even mentioned in
an earlier work talking about

00:54:14.800 --> 00:54:16.310
the value of expertise.

00:54:16.310 --> 00:54:20.170
Like how would you speak to
the need to retain the norms

00:54:20.170 --> 00:54:24.397
as we're transitioning into
these digitalized maps?

00:54:26.332 --> 00:54:27.708
- I think it's a really
important question,

00:54:27.708 --> 00:54:29.940
that's why I only listen
to music on vinyl,

00:54:29.940 --> 00:54:31.870
to avoid any of that
quantization that will

00:54:31.870 --> 00:54:34.051
just totally destroy the sound.

00:54:34.051 --> 00:54:36.980
There's been really interesting
efforts there, there's,

00:54:36.980 --> 00:54:39.580
I might mispronounce this, but
Mukurtu, which is an effort

00:54:39.580 --> 00:54:42.400
to build a content management
system for the GLAM space

00:54:42.400 --> 00:54:46.379
which is, originates more
in native understandings of

00:54:46.379 --> 00:54:48.671
knowledge or restriction of knowledge

00:54:48.671 --> 00:54:50.729
or empowerment of knowledge.

00:54:50.729 --> 00:54:53.320
That's something I think
a lot of folks including,

00:54:53.320 --> 00:54:55.360
I just got back from Australia
where people are really

00:54:55.360 --> 00:54:57.750
thinking about that in
the aboriginal context

00:54:57.750 --> 00:54:59.478
or a Strait Islanders context.

00:54:59.478 --> 00:55:02.360
The great thing, the wonderful
thing and the horrible thing

00:55:02.360 --> 00:55:04.240
about the digital is
that, you know, you can

00:55:04.240 --> 00:55:05.440
build whatever you want with it.

00:55:05.440 --> 00:55:08.920
So if you want a system that
will replicate existing systems

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:11.240
of knowledge control you
can do that, you can also do

00:55:11.240 --> 00:55:14.580
sort of liberative or sort
of transcendent approaches

00:55:14.580 --> 00:55:18.127
that liberate information or
present it in different ways.

00:55:18.127 --> 00:55:21.240
I do think it's a fascinating
question you asked about

00:55:21.240 --> 00:55:23.300
the notion of the
quantization or the reduction.

00:55:23.300 --> 00:55:25.584
I think its the responsibility
of every human is to say,

00:55:25.584 --> 00:55:27.704
does the particular
model I'm working with,

00:55:27.704 --> 00:55:30.920
whether that's ASCII or Unicode,
whether that's 8-bit color

00:55:30.920 --> 00:55:34.670
or 24-bit color, a vinyl or
CD, you know it's inherently

00:55:34.670 --> 00:55:37.300
gonna be a model of the truth.

00:55:37.300 --> 00:55:40.490
And so the question is, the
thing people heard a lot

00:55:40.490 --> 00:55:42.780
is all models are wrong,
some of them are useful.

00:55:42.780 --> 00:55:45.289
So you as a humanist have
to decide is the model

00:55:45.289 --> 00:55:48.690
I'm working with, you know,
the parallel of the GAN versus

00:55:48.690 --> 00:55:51.730
the human sort of schizophrenia,
is that a useful model,

00:55:51.730 --> 00:55:53.833
you know, for your
particular research quest?

00:55:58.560 --> 00:56:00.433
- Thank you so much for
this fascinating question,

00:56:00.433 --> 00:56:03.966
and if I may, just a quick story,

00:56:03.966 --> 00:56:08.966
because, yes, quantization is
breaking some kind of nuances,

00:56:13.000 --> 00:56:16.670
but if you add enough information maybe

00:56:16.670 --> 00:56:17.940
you can get even more nuance.

00:56:17.940 --> 00:56:22.020
What I mean by that is I'm
working on early modern books,

00:56:22.020 --> 00:56:25.230
and early modern books are
by definition hard to find.

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:28.600
And I did my PhD in Switzerland
where there were not as much

00:56:28.600 --> 00:56:31.090
early modern French book as in Paris.

00:56:31.090 --> 00:56:35.770
So for a long time everybody
doing a PhD in Switzerland

00:56:35.770 --> 00:56:39.080
on early modern French
literature had to work on only

00:56:39.080 --> 00:56:41.290
some authors just because there was only

00:56:41.290 --> 00:56:44.210
modern editions of them existing.

00:56:44.210 --> 00:56:47.049
So it was basically white males' work.

00:56:47.049 --> 00:56:49.366
Right, when I started my PhD I was able

00:56:49.366 --> 00:56:51.640
to access Google Books.

00:56:51.640 --> 00:56:54.366
So suddenly, there was a whole
lot of books that weren't

00:56:54.366 --> 00:56:57.639
available except if you
were living in Paris.

00:56:57.639 --> 00:57:01.820
To me, here in Switzerland,
or to anyone, and also

00:57:01.820 --> 00:57:05.310
a whole new lot of authors
and the texts were not only

00:57:05.310 --> 00:57:07.560
available like the text, but in a

00:57:07.560 --> 00:57:09.220
photographic reproduction of the page.

00:57:09.220 --> 00:57:11.690
So a lot of things that were reserved

00:57:11.690 --> 00:57:14.350
to a very specific elite before,

00:57:14.350 --> 00:57:17.010
were available to the entire world.

00:57:17.010 --> 00:57:18.519
I am putting big quotes here, but,

00:57:18.519 --> 00:57:21.490
we understand what we are saying there.

00:57:21.490 --> 00:57:24.361
So suddenly a lot of new
authors came into the field

00:57:24.361 --> 00:57:29.361
and went back into the study,
and this expertise that was

00:57:29.799 --> 00:57:34.799
previously reserved to
only just a few people

00:57:34.970 --> 00:57:38.740
that were able to access these
book in the specific library,

00:57:38.740 --> 00:57:41.280
were available and raised
new questions et cetera.

00:57:41.280 --> 00:57:44.030
So, there is this kind
of, we can speak about,

00:57:44.030 --> 00:57:46.582
yeah but we don't access the
actual book, and it's true

00:57:46.582 --> 00:57:50.753
and at the same time
through this quantization,

00:57:52.430 --> 00:57:56.897
we open the access in a
very different way, so.

00:57:58.030 --> 00:58:01.440
- Just a further comment on
the ideas of reductionism

00:58:01.440 --> 00:58:03.360
and models and that really beautiful

00:58:03.360 --> 00:58:06.318
George Box quote about usefulness.

00:58:06.318 --> 00:58:09.710
We learned a lot about
how airplanes can fly by

00:58:09.710 --> 00:58:13.080
making balsa wood wings and
placing them in a wind tunnel,

00:58:13.080 --> 00:58:15.070
and we never got mad at
ourselves that we couldn't

00:58:15.070 --> 00:58:18.340
cram our luggage into that
wing and take off and leave,

00:58:18.340 --> 00:58:20.610
like we're not aiming for
complete descriptions,

00:58:20.610 --> 00:58:23.530
we're aiming, well ultimately
we want a complete description

00:58:23.530 --> 00:58:25.997
but our pathway there
is breaking things down

00:58:25.997 --> 00:58:27.480
and putting them back together again.

00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:29.730
And I think that's what
we're all engaged in.

00:58:32.893 --> 00:58:35.145
- Thank you again,
that's a great question.

00:58:35.145 --> 00:58:38.900
I think a lot about the
issues of knowledge equity

00:58:38.900 --> 00:58:41.420
and, you know, who is
considered an expert.

00:58:41.420 --> 00:58:43.700
This is something that is kind of an issue

00:58:43.700 --> 00:58:47.168
for Wikipedia right now,
working on a sort of strategy

00:58:47.168 --> 00:58:48.893
for the next few decades.

00:58:49.860 --> 00:58:52.780
If you've ever tried to edit
Wikipedia you have come into

00:58:52.780 --> 00:58:55.050
knowledge it's actually not that easy.

00:58:55.050 --> 00:58:57.810
So you can try to edit,
but if you, for example,

00:58:57.810 --> 00:59:00.843
add something without a
reference, it'll get deleted.

00:59:00.843 --> 00:59:04.637
Or if your reference is
deemed not expert enough,

00:59:04.637 --> 00:59:07.842
or not legitimate enough you
might get deleted as well.

00:59:07.842 --> 00:59:10.475
So this is a problem because
there are whole communities

00:59:10.475 --> 00:59:12.450
around the world that, for example,

00:59:12.450 --> 00:59:15.680
don't have the same idea of expertise.

00:59:15.680 --> 00:59:17.863
Maybe their knowledge
is mostly oral history,

00:59:17.863 --> 00:59:21.177
maybe they don't have as many
written records as we do.

00:59:21.177 --> 00:59:24.120
So it's a question for the
Wikipedia right now, and for,

00:59:24.120 --> 00:59:28.850
I'm sure, all of us, about
how we look at those types of

00:59:28.850 --> 00:59:31.640
knowledge and how we evaluate
who gets to be an expert

00:59:31.640 --> 00:59:35.170
in the room, who gets to sit
on a panel at Yale University

00:59:35.170 --> 00:59:38.240
talking about what knowledge is, right?

00:59:38.240 --> 00:59:41.940
We don't have anyone from those
communities who may not have

00:59:41.940 --> 00:59:45.305
the benefits of multiple
degrees from fancy universities

00:59:45.305 --> 00:59:47.507
in order to talk about these things.

00:59:47.507 --> 00:59:50.480
And if they're not in
the conversation then

00:59:50.480 --> 00:59:53.580
their knowledge is lost and
knowledge doesn't get to them.

00:59:53.580 --> 00:59:55.500
So that's something I think
we have to think about

00:59:55.500 --> 00:59:56.513
a lot as well too.

00:59:59.400 --> 01:00:00.590
- Thank you for your
question and thank you

01:00:00.590 --> 01:00:02.420
for the wonderful responses.

01:00:02.420 --> 01:00:05.121
I'd like one last applause
for our round table speakers.

01:00:05.121 --> 01:00:07.371
(applause)

01:00:12.838 --> 01:00:15.080
and I'd like to invite Susan Gibbons up

01:00:15.080 --> 01:00:16.503
to give closing remarks.

01:00:22.150 --> 01:00:24.170
- Good afternoon everyone,
I'm Susan Gibbons

01:00:24.170 --> 01:00:26.866
and I have the honor of being
Yale's University librarian

01:00:26.866 --> 01:00:28.469
and Deputy Provost for Collections

01:00:28.469 --> 01:00:30.890
and Scholarly Communication.

01:00:30.890 --> 01:00:33.793
It's also been my pleasure
to have been able to attend

01:00:33.793 --> 01:00:35.984
these conferences, this is now our fourth,

01:00:35.984 --> 01:00:40.210
and therefore can comment on
the arc of the conferences

01:00:40.210 --> 01:00:42.367
and how they have changed over time.

01:00:42.367 --> 01:00:45.795
Initially, with our first
conference we had a lot of

01:00:45.795 --> 01:00:47.390
speakers from the outside.

01:00:47.390 --> 01:00:50.530
We brought them in to help
get the conversation started.

01:00:50.530 --> 01:00:54.409
Today we're able to fill the
room and fill the program

01:00:54.409 --> 01:00:57.150
with conversations from
inside of Yale, because

01:00:57.150 --> 01:01:00.210
we've come so far and there's
so many robust projects

01:01:00.210 --> 01:01:01.698
to be talked about.

01:01:01.698 --> 01:01:04.530
If you look at the program
today we had speakers who are

01:01:04.530 --> 01:01:08.270
undergraduates, graduate
students, faculty, and librarians,

01:01:08.270 --> 01:01:10.970
and I'd say there aren't too
many conferences that happen

01:01:10.970 --> 01:01:14.206
on campus that have that
mix within that program.

01:01:14.206 --> 01:01:16.640
Similarly if you look
at the disciplines that

01:01:16.640 --> 01:01:19.686
were represented, math,
data science, law, French,

01:01:19.686 --> 01:01:22.720
engineering, music,
psychiatry, and many more.

01:01:22.720 --> 01:01:25.666
Again, where else do we have
these kinds of opportunities

01:01:25.666 --> 01:01:28.210
where we can talk across
disciplines and talk

01:01:28.210 --> 01:01:30.740
in meaningful ways that
we can actually connect

01:01:30.740 --> 01:01:32.880
across the different disciplines?

01:01:32.880 --> 01:01:35.249
We started this conference as a way

01:01:35.249 --> 01:01:38.240
to cause these conversations to happen.

01:01:38.240 --> 01:01:41.320
To help share the fact that
there were common methods

01:01:41.320 --> 01:01:44.820
and tools and data acquisition methods

01:01:44.820 --> 01:01:49.820
and methods for demonstrating
and visualizing our findings,

01:01:50.090 --> 01:01:53.160
that they were happening
throughout various disciplines,

01:01:53.160 --> 01:01:55.210
but you weren't always recognizing them.

01:01:55.210 --> 01:01:57.920
And so what we wanted this
conference to do was to

01:01:57.920 --> 01:02:01.610
give an opportunity to share
ideas, to share projects,

01:02:01.610 --> 01:02:04.781
and realize that you actually
have an intellectual partner

01:02:04.781 --> 01:02:08.398
that's somewhere across this
campus that can form a network

01:02:08.398 --> 01:02:12.010
essentially to help you move
forward with your projects.

01:02:12.010 --> 01:02:14.414
And so I think we have done that.

01:02:14.414 --> 01:02:17.031
The last piece I'll observe though is

01:02:17.031 --> 01:02:20.792
in our early conferences
it was a speaker presenting

01:02:20.792 --> 01:02:24.340
on a project, one after
another, which was wonderful.

01:02:24.340 --> 01:02:27.220
But we weren't yet ready to
have the kinds of conversations

01:02:27.220 --> 01:02:28.810
we just had with this panel.

01:02:28.810 --> 01:02:32.112
That we could actually
have the kind of discussion

01:02:32.112 --> 01:02:35.100
that we did that goes
beyond the technology

01:02:35.100 --> 01:02:37.760
and goes beyond the
techniques and asks some of

01:02:37.760 --> 01:02:40.450
the deeper questions that
regardless of discipline

01:02:40.450 --> 01:02:42.477
we all need to be thinking about.

01:02:42.477 --> 01:02:45.930
And that's a maturation of
the conversation here at Yale

01:02:45.930 --> 01:02:49.140
that is just so wonderful
to see and experience.

01:02:49.140 --> 01:02:51.271
So we will continue to
try to bring together

01:02:51.271 --> 01:02:54.290
these conversations and invite even

01:02:54.290 --> 01:02:57.170
a wider and wider audience to participate.

01:02:57.170 --> 01:03:00.501
So before we transition
to our poster session,

01:03:00.501 --> 01:03:02.770
which will be right next door,

01:03:02.770 --> 01:03:05.690
I wanna thank our different sponsors.

01:03:05.690 --> 01:03:08.484
We have Yale STEAM, Yale Code4Good,

01:03:08.484 --> 01:03:11.200
Yale's Digital Humanities Lab, and

01:03:11.200 --> 01:03:13.410
the Yale Poorvu Center
for Teaching and Learning.

01:03:13.410 --> 01:03:16.059
Throughout the day they
have contributed in

01:03:16.059 --> 01:03:17.060
many different ways and
so if you would join me

01:03:17.060 --> 01:03:18.868
in a round of applause for our sponsors.

01:03:18.868 --> 01:03:21.118
(applause)

